  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Inside the luxury suite, drunk Nagiyev has been hiding out and drinking with the bellboy. When the commission walks in, Nagiyev happily offers to give them a tour of the soft sofas while Mikhail desperately tries to hustle the inspectors out the door.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Извините, что мы вас побеспокоили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Комиссия в отеле. Мы сейчас уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "О, комиссия! Так я вам сейчас всё покажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот диванчики мягкие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ой, только он пустой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, пойдёмте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пойдём. Ну, куда вы уходите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Подождите! Ой, извините.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Что здесь происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У них на родине сухой закон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас всё будет хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Идёмте дальше.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Извините, что мы вас побеспокоили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse us for disturbing you.' In Russian, the idea of 'excuse me' flows naturally into 'disturbed' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Комиссия в отеле. Мы сейчас уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The commission is in the hotel. We are leaving right now.' The meaning hinges on 'commission' working together with 'are leaving' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "О, комиссия! Так я вам сейчас всё покажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, the commission! Well I will show you everything right now.' The meaning hinges on 'about' working together with 'will show' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот диванчики мягкие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here the little sofas are soft.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'here' and finishing on 'soft'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ой, только он пустой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, only it is empty.' Notice how Russian expresses 'oh' and 'empty' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, пойдёмте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, let us go.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'yes' before landing on 'let's go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пойдём. Ну, куда вы уходите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us go. Well, where are you going?' In Russian, the idea of 'let's go' flows naturally into 'are leaving' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Подождите! Ой, извините.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait! Oh, excuse me.' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'wait', while 'excuse me' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Что здесь происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is going on here?' The grammar packs 'what' and 'is happening' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У них на родине сухой закон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In their homeland there is prohibition.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'law' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас всё будет хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Right now everything will be fine.' In Russian, the idea of 'now' flows naturally into 'well' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Идёмте дальше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us move on.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'let us go' and finishing on 'further'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
